 "summary": "The Lean theorem states five facts about the Koebe map k(z)=z/(1−z)^2. It is schlicht on the unit disk. ∫_D |k'|^{-2} dA = ∞ and ∫_D |k'|^{2/3} dA = ∞. Its inverse g on the Koebe domain Ω = k(D) satisfies |g'|^4 ∉ L¹(Ω) and |g'|^{4/3} ∉ L¹(Ω). This is the classical sharpness example for the Brennan exponent range 4/3 < p < 4. The most important caveat: none of this appears in the supplied excerpt. That paper's Theorem 1.1, the uniform M_{-1}[f'](r) bound giving B_b(−1) < 1/4, is not formalized at all. The Lean content apparently belongs to the cited companion article on Brennan's conjecture, which is not in the excerpt.",
